---------- Forwarded message ---------
From: clasy...@gmail.com <Unknown>
Date: Friday, May 18, 2018 at 5:01:25 AM UTC-5
Subject: Alternate release of the SIMH component of PEPS for Windows
To: pepswin <Unknown>


I'm not an expert on the inner machinations of the SIMH engine; my 
expertise is dealing with the vagaries of the various Windows releases and 
all things 12-bits.

It has come to my attention that there is an alternate method of compiling 
the SIMH source code into a complete .exe file that does NOT require the 
cygwin1.dll file at all.

Moreover, The Cygwin project is dropping support for Windows XP and Windows 
Vista in the latest releases.  The last known compatible release is 
available for download from their website, etc.

Thus, the newer method is welcome here, so we don't have to deal with this 
project as a bastard step-child of the unix-oriented release.

Apparently, the necessary tools are all Microsoft all the time eliminating 
any mention of GCC from the instructions.

I would appreciate any follow-up posts on this, as there best release 
should be used in the next [hopefully stable] release of PEPS for Windows.

There are a couple of non-standard parameters to be used in the source 
code, which has a lot of maintenance problems of late, so I am asking for 
volunteers for this:

1) The release presently is an old enough compilation that requires 
cygwin1.dll that runs from Windows XP and Windows Vista.   As such, this is 
not a crisis per se.

2) Dave Gesswein made some form of temporary patch to this version to allow 
the behavior of TC08 DECtape support to have a "magical" property that is 
of benefit in both OS/8 and P?S/8, but it is of more useful purpose in 
P?S/8 [more below].

3) I have received a newer copy in binary from Dave that is supposed to 
extend analogous support to the TD8E, but it has not been thoroughly tested 
at this point.  Fortunately, the only current benefit would be the 
equivalent one in OS/8 to the small benefit explained below for the TC08 
support; There is no current P?S/8 support for the TD8E; that said, there 
WILL be once a lot of now-possible recovery is completed.  I consider 
getting the TC08 version more up to speed more important, especially since 
in the SIMH environment, the TD8E support is slower and not as complete and 
more unwieldy if you want more than two drives.  This will be explained 
later in a separate document that will also be cross-posted into the P?S/8 
group.  Some other limitations of the SIMH program are required to even 
consider this as a practical matter; reasonable configurations of OS/8 have 
liittle concern for this, thus primarily this ;limitation is a result of 
disuse; when P/S/8 is more activelly developed with regard to the TD8E, 
hopefully someone can revisit the TD9E support in SIMH to do it up properly.

Now to the aforementioned feature:

Those of you who used real DECtapes back in the day may be familiar with 
the use of "extra-long" DECtapes.  For years, while sold tape media was 
NOMINALLY 260 feet long, the tape was OFTEN just enough longer to justify 
creation of longer tapes.

Using the P?S/8 self-patching version of the TOG-8 DECtape formatting 
program for the TC01 and TC08, you can specify additional blocks; a 
practical semi-standard arose where they are a noticeable number of tapes 
not limited to the standard 2702 octal blocks, but actually 3300 octal 
blocks.  This exact number was chosen because it represented a nice "round" 
number in P?S/8 terms that has influence on the TFS directory capacity; 
OS/8 of course only cares about even numbers of blocks.  Thus, this number 
is a good value for both systems and a serious number of actual real-world 
DECtapes exist and the question was how to support them in SIMH.

All of that said, Dave and I worked out an additional feature in SIMH to 
take additional advantage of, allowing up to 10000 octal [4096] DECtape 
blocks.

When set that high, it is impossible for any block value attempted to 
return a a forward END error, and as a practical matter, SIMH thus allows 
as many blocks as you wish [up to 4096].

As a practical matter, in the realm of OS/8 this is merely interesting; 
Still longer devices are often one of the half-pack devices of the OS/8 
RK8E support.  However, a  4096 block long DECtape is one half the maximum 
theoretical size of any one OS/8 logical device; the RK8E devices are only 
around 2/3 of this value, thus they are actually fairly close in capacity.  
thus, if you want to configure OS/8 to support DECtape anyway, you get the 
added benefit of a sizable "fantasy" DECtape which means that the meager 
overall count of maximum number of OS/8 devices at any one time gets a 
slight boost in overall storage capacity should you need it; this is 
admittedly by itself not a major point, but it is a valid one [and more of 
use to those of us who use both OS/8 and P?S/8; the TC08 support allows one 
overall configuration to support both systems reasonable without one system 
compromising the other, despite the present support for only TC08 
presently. [Note:  P?S/s is NOT limited to only TC08 support, but due to 
circumstances gradually changing, this will appear later;  Running on 
fantasy DECtapes is perfectly fine for the present; future releases of 
P?S/8 will more depend on still larger structures, but for the present, an 
ideal configuration is for OS/8 to boot on drive 0 of the RK8E and P?S/8 to 
boot from drive 0 of the TC08 DECtape.  That said, there wil more sooner be 
P?S8 also capable of booting on the RK8E, and that will be solved by using 
the more general RK8E bootstrap to any of the four drives known in SIMH as 
RK0, RK1, RK2, and RK3.

In P?S/8 for the TC08, there is only one caveat:

Real-world DECtapes are never as long as block 3777.  As such, it is 
perfectly fine to use 11-bit arithmetic in the tape search algorithm.  the 
P?S/8 system device handler and companion non-system device handler both 
use 12-bit search [as does OS/8] so for all of this software, there is no 
problem.

However, P?S/8 OFTEN [but not always] supports a concept beyond OS/8's 
basic design, the device-dependent binary loader.  [Note: This loader was 
named the "slurp" loader by Richard Lary who also started OS/8.  it first 
appeared in the R-L Monitor System, the logical "grandfather" of P?S/8; 
that system only ran on a single drive 0 of th eTC01 or TC08 while P?S/8 
supports all 8 logical units from the system device handler; thus much of 
P?S/8 only requires the system handler and thus no overhead of handler 
loading, etc.  You are limited to the 8 logical devices, but the 
performance is palpably better in P?S/8.  [doubters have come away 
believers when trying it for themselves].

The TC08 supports the slurp loader, but the code is very tight within this 
code, which is NOT a device handler, rather a much more efficient binary 
file loader explicitly.  When it is running, the P?S/9 system device 
handler is kicked out of memory and the slurp loader replaces it.  for full 
compatibility, restart at 07600 does reload P?S/8, thus the design requires 
that as well.  This allows unrestricted binary file loading into any 
location in the range of 00000-07577, the same as OS/8 in that regard, and 
any existent extended memory location as well.  [Unlike OS/8, there is no 
general reservation of x7600-x7777 for any extended memory field x.]

Also, there is a neat trick:  By passing a special "file" to the slurp 
loader, after all of the intended user files are loaded, it is possible to 
reload the system handler!  All of this is done with all drives 
write-protected.  This sounds counter-intuitive, but to those intimately 
familiar with the design of the slurp loader and the P?S/8 TFS file 
structure and other arcane deals, suddenly the impossible becomes reality.  
Anyone actually interested be prepared to have many of the notions you have 
about PDP-8 operating systems to be severely shattered.  [And that's likely 
a good thing!].

Due to the 11-bit coding of the search algorithim, it is not possible to 
access past block 3777; the code will crash.

Fortunately, there is a remedy:  ALL P?S/8 systems support a VIRTUAL slurp 
loader which is analogous to OS/8's ABSLDR program.  While the P?S/8 
virtual loader is much slower than the non-virtual slurp loader, it always 
works as long as the system device handler does [since it uses it].  thus, 
the only practical change is that commands to the P?S/8 BIN utility program 
must use the /V command-line switch should the referenced files be block 
4000 or higher.  Even in P?S/8 this is not a likely aspect of the 
configuration, but it is theoretically possible.

thus, the theoreticals aside, P?S/8 effectively supports 8 DECtapes that 
are each about three times the size as the normal real-world limit, 
assuming the configuration is jiggered accordingly.  As in OS/8, small 
changes have to be made to the directory structures; the specific changes 
are system dependent and P?S/8 considerations are a bit more than OS/8.

However, when configured as such, P?S/8 becomes much more capable as a 
practical matter, in most ways, the provided P?S/8 configuration thus 
exceed even the RK8E configuration in certain ways!  This all stems from 
the fact that the system device handler includes support for up to 8 
logical units while the OS/8 system device handler can only handle the boot 
device; non-system handlers must be used to access additional devices.

When P?S/8 is released supporting the TD8E, it will be time to ensure the 
TD8E parallel support also works.  But for the moment, the only practical 
improvement will be the speed of SIMH as long as the TC08 length parameter 
is set the same way in the recompiled source release of SIMH.

cjl

